        <h2>Why most AI rollouts stall</h2>
        <p>
          AI adoption fails for the same reasons any software adoption fails. The tool is rarely the problem. The gaps are almost always in four places:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>No specific use case.</strong> "Use AI more" is not a plan. People try it once, get a generic answer, and go back to doing it the old way.</li>
          <li><strong>No guardrails.</strong> Nobody knows what data is allowed in which tool, so the careful people avoid it and the careless people paste in client files.</li>
          <li><strong>Training by tool, not by role.</strong> A one-hour "intro to ChatGPT" session doesn't show an estimator how to check a scope or a PM how to turn meeting notes into an RFI log.</li>
          <li><strong>No measurement.</strong> Without a before-and-after number, AI stays a novelty instead of a line item you can defend.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The five steps below close those gaps in order. Most small teams can run the first three in a month.
        </p>

        <h2>Step 1: Find where the hours actually go</h2>
        <p>
          Before you pick a tool, pick the work. For one week, have each role jot down the repetitive writing, reading, and summarizing tasks they do and roughly how long each takes. You're looking for work that is frequent, text-heavy, and follows a pattern.
        </p>
        <p>
          In most small businesses the same candidates show up:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>First drafts of proposals, estimates narratives, and scopes of work</li>
          <li>Meeting notes turned into action items and follow-up emails</li>
          <li>Summaries of long documents: contracts, specs, RFPs, policies</li>
          <li>Routine customer and vendor email replies</li>
          <li>Weekly status reports built from data you already have</li>
          <li>Writing SOPs and checklists from how the work is really done today</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Step 2: Score the use cases, then pick two or three</h2>
        <p>
          Rank every candidate on two questions. How many hours a month would it save across the team? And what's the damage if the AI gets it wrong and nobody catches it? Start with work that scores high on the first and low on the second.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Good first use cases:</strong> drafts a person always reviews before sending, internal summaries, meeting notes, first-pass SOPs, and research you'd verify anyway.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Hold for later:</strong> anything that makes a final call on money, safety, legal terms, or eligibility, and anything that requires putting sensitive client data into a tool you haven't vetted. These can come later, with tighter controls. They're a bad place to learn.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two or three use cases done well beat twenty done halfway. Pick the few that save the most hours and put a named owner on each.
        </p>

        <h2>Step 3: Write a one-page acceptable-use policy</h2>
        <p>
          This is the step most small businesses skip, and it's the one that protects you. It doesn't need to be long. One page that answers five questions is enough:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Which tools are approved?</strong> Name them. Business plans typically offer stronger data controls than free personal accounts, so read each vendor's data terms before you approve it.</li>
          <li><strong>What data never goes in?</strong> Client confidential information, personal data, financial account details, and anything covered by a contract or regulation, unless the tool is approved for it.</li>
          <li><strong>Who reviews the output?</strong> A person checks every AI draft before it reaches a client, a regulator, or a bank. AI output can sound confident and still be wrong.</li>
          <li><strong>When do you tell clients?</strong> Decide your disclosure rule up front, especially for deliverables clients pay for.</li>
          <li><strong>Who owns the policy?</strong> One person keeps the approved-tool list current as vendors change their terms and features.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Step 4: Train by role, not by tool</h2>
        <p>
          Generic AI training produces generic results. Build short sessions around the actual use cases from Step 2, using your own documents and your own formats. An estimator learns to check a scope against the drawings list. An office manager learns to turn a voicemail transcript into a job ticket. A PM learns to draft the weekly owner update from the daily logs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Give each role a small prompt library: five to ten tested prompts for their real tasks, stored where everyone can find them and improve them. The library matters more than the training session. It's what people reach for on a busy Tuesday.
        </p>

        <h2>Step 5: Measure what it saves</h2>
        <p>
          Go back to the time estimates from Step 1. After 30 days, ask the same people how long the same tasks take now, and check adoption: who is actually using the approved tools, and for what. If a use case isn't saving time, find out why. Usually it's the prompt, the review step, or the fact that nobody owns it.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is what turns AI from a subscription into an operating improvement. When you can say "proposals take four hours instead of seven," you can decide whether to expand, and to where.
        </p>

        <h2>The mistakes worth avoiding</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Buying a new AI platform first.</strong> The AI inside the tools you already pay for (email, office suite, PM software) is often enough to start.</li>
          <li><strong>Letting one enthusiast carry it.</strong> If the only person who knows the prompts leaves, the program leaves with them. Document it.</li>
          <li><strong>Skipping the review step to save time.</strong> The time savings come from faster first drafts, not from removing the person who checks the work.</li>
          <li><strong>Banning it outright.</strong> People use it anyway, just without guardrails. A clear policy is safer than a ban nobody follows.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What to run yourself vs. what to bring help on</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Run it yourself if:</strong> you have fewer than a dozen people, one clear use case, and someone with the time to own the policy and the prompt library for 60 days. The steps above are the whole method.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Bring help if:</strong>
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>You handle client, patient, or financial data and need guardrails you can defend.</li>
          <li>You've tried rolling out AI once and usage faded after a few weeks.</li>
          <li>You want AI connected to your existing systems (PM software, CRM, accounting), not just used in a chat window.</li>
          <li>You need a measured result you can show a partner, a board, or a lender.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="mt-10 text-sm text-ink-mute">
          <em>Tyler Alexander is the founder of Cranes Consulting in Tampa, FL. If you want a second opinion on where AI would save your team the most time, set up a 30-minute call. No deck, no pitch.</em>
        </p>
